WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.841
Begin by framing Workflow Architecture as a
question about inputs, decisions, and

2
00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:09.140
observable results.

3
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:16.435
Data-analysis workflows separate data
preparation, exploratory analysis, training,

4
00:00:16.435 --> 00:00:21.906
validation, testing, model selection, and
evaluation.

5
00:00:21.906 --> 00:00:30.773
Consider this worked case: A pipeline begins
by cleaning raw CSV files, then splits them

6
00:00:30.773 --> 00:00:36.093
into training and validation sets before
fitting a classifier.

7
00:00:36.093 --> 00:00:43.079
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

8
00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:43.578
conclusion.

9
00:00:43.578 --> 00:00:49.124
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

10
00:00:49.226 --> 00:00:56.074
Now define Supervised Learning Foundations
precisely enough that another learner could

11
00:00:56.074 --> 00:00:57.941
test the definition.

12
00:00:57.941 --> 00:01:05.079
Supervised methods learn mappings from
examples paired with target labels, while

13
00:01:05.079 --> 00:01:08.972
unsupervised methods identify structure
without targets.

14
00:01:08.972 --> 00:01:15.927
Consider this worked case: Training a logistic
regression model on labeled spam emails where

15
00:01:15.927 --> 00:01:20.894
the label indicates whether each message is
spam or not.

16
00:01:20.894 --> 00:01:27.322
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

17
00:01:27.322 --> 00:01:29.660
evidence would distinguish them.

18
00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:36.129
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

19
00:01:36.242 --> 00:01:43.689
With the foundation in place, examine how
Unsupervised Learning Foundations operates in

20
00:01:43.689 --> 00:01:45.551
a concrete sequence.

21
00:01:45.551 --> 00:01:52.326
Query planning and physical storage affect
performance without changing logical query

22
00:01:52.326 --> 00:01:52.942
meaning.

23
00:01:52.942 --> 00:02:00.518
Consider this worked case: Applying k-means
clustering to customer purchase data to group

24
00:02:00.518 --> 00:02:04.598
users by spending patterns without predefined
categories.

25
00:02:04.598 --> 00:02:12.207
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

26
00:02:12.207 --> 00:02:17.754
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

27
00:02:17.754 --> 00:02:24.906
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

28
00:02:24.906 --> 00:02:27.051
throughout the analysis.

29
00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:33.478
Next, compare the choices involved in
Preprocessing Constraints and make the

30
00:02:33.478 --> 00:02:35.198
decision criteria explicit.

31
00:02:35.198 --> 00:02:41.901
Preprocessing must be fitted only on permitted
training data to avoid leakage.

32
00:02:41.901 --> 00:02:48.651
Consider this worked case: Scaling features
using the mean and standard deviation

33
00:02:48.651 --> 00:02:54.838
calculated exclusively from the training set
before applying to validation data.

34
00:02:54.838 --> 00:03:01.923
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

35
00:03:01.923 --> 00:03:03.104
the goalposts.

36
00:03:03.104 --> 00:03:09.948
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

37
00:03:10.058 --> 00:03:17.178
Apply Pipeline Consistency to a worked
situation instead of treating it as an

38
00:03:17.178 --> 00:03:18.273
isolated definition.

39
00:03:18.273 --> 00:03:26.309
Pipelines combine transformations and
estimators so the same operations are applied

40
00:03:26.309 --> 00:03:27.039
consistently.

41
00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:33.854
Consider this worked case: A single pipeline
object that chains standardization,

42
00:03:33.854 --> 00:03:40.669
imputation, and model fitting, ensuring
identical operations run during training and

43
00:03:40.669 --> 00:03:41.289
prediction.

44
00:03:41.289 --> 00:03:48.891
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

45
00:03:48.891 --> 00:03:49.398
step.

46
00:03:49.398 --> 00:03:54.492
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

47
00:03:54.602 --> 00:04:01.131
Examine the tradeoffs in Cross-Validation
Mechanics, including what improves and what

48
00:04:01.131 --> 00:04:02.911
becomes more difficult.

49
00:04:02.911 --> 00:04:09.817
Cross-validation repeats training and
validation across defined splits.

50
00:04:09.817 --> 00:04:15.654
Consider this worked case: Using five-fold
cross-validation where the dataset is

51
00:04:15.654 --> 00:04:23.615
partitioned into five subsets, each serving as
a validation set once while the others train

52
00:04:23.615 --> 00:04:24.676
the model.

53
00:04:24.677 --> 00:04:31.239
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

54
00:04:31.239 --> 00:04:38.551
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

55
00:04:38.666 --> 00:04:46.131
Stress-test the reasoning around Evaluation
Alignment with an edge case and identify which

56
00:04:46.131 --> 00:04:48.428
assumption controls the outcome.

57
00:04:48.428 --> 00:04:57.159
Evaluation must match the task and the cost of
errors, while distribution shift, missing

58
00:04:57.159 --> 00:05:02.772
data, overfitting, and unexamined sampling
choices can invalidate conclusions.

59
00:05:02.772 --> 00:05:10.137
Consider this worked case: Using precision and
recall for fraud detection because false

60
00:05:10.137 --> 00:05:16.936
negatives carry higher cost than false
positives, and adjusting thresholds based on

61
00:05:16.936 --> 00:05:18.069
class imbalance.

62
00:05:18.069 --> 00:05:24.537
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

63
00:05:24.537 --> 00:05:31.600
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

64
00:05:31.706 --> 00:05:39.178
Use Data Quality Analysis to diagnose a
failure by separating symptoms, causes, and

65
00:05:39.178 --> 00:05:40.327
corrective action.

66
00:05:40.327 --> 00:05:47.294
Data quality analysis makes missingness,
duplicates, inconsistent representation,

67
00:05:47.294 --> 00:05:53.390
integrity constraints, sampling, and update
behavior explicit.

68
00:05:53.390 --> 00:06:00.534
Consider this worked case: Running a data
profiling script that reports null counts,

69
00:06:00.534 --> 00:06:06.030
duplicate row percentages, and value
distribution shifts across time periods.

70
00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:13.789
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

71
00:06:13.789 --> 00:06:15.452
smallest plausible cause.

72
00:06:15.452 --> 00:06:22.749
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

73
00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:29.865
Connect Query and Storage Impact to the
earlier ideas and explain how the pieces work

74
00:06:29.865 --> 00:06:31.268
as one system.

75
00:06:31.268 --> 00:06:37.581
Queries select, join, aggregate, order, and
transform data.

76
00:06:37.581 --> 00:06:44.808
Consider this worked case: Adding a B-tree
index on a frequently filtered column speeds

77
00:06:44.808 --> 00:06:50.487
up SELECT operations while preserving the
exact same SQL result set.

78
00:06:50.487 --> 00:07:00.197
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

79
00:07:00.197 --> 00:07:01.815
across representations.

80
00:07:01.815 --> 00:07:09.424
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

81
00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:16.512
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

82
00:07:16.512 --> 00:07:17.011
analysis.

83
00:07:17.011 --> 00:07:24.501
Analytical work should preserve provenance
from captured data through cleaning,

84
00:07:24.501 --> 00:07:28.995
transformation, grouping, modeling,
visualization, and reporting.

85
00:07:28.995 --> 00:07:36.837
Consider this worked case: Maintaining a
versioned data lineage log that tracks each

86
00:07:36.837 --> 00:07:42.267
transformation step, model parameters, and
visualization settings for reproducible

87
00:07:42.267 --> 00:07:42.870
research.

88
00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:51.158
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

89
00:07:51.158 --> 00:07:52.433
verification step.

90
00:07:52.433 --> 00:07:59.301
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

91
00:07:59.301 --> 00:07:59.792
intuition.
